1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,009
A heart is placed on a scale.

2
00:00:04,309 --> 00:00:09,878
On the other side sits a single feather,
the sign of Maat, truth and right order.

3
00:00:10,178 --> 00:00:14,524
People often say that the heart
had to be lighter than the feather,

4
00:00:14,524 --> 00:00:17,685
and that if it failed,
a monster would devour it.

5
00:00:17,985 --> 00:00:22,911
This is the famous Weighing of the Heart,
from the Egyptian Book of the Dead.

6
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:26,125
It sounds like a terrifying final exam.

7
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:29,039
But what does the book
itself actually say?

8
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:38,367
In this episode, we open the
Book of the Dead itself: its real name,

9
00:00:38,367 --> 00:00:43,813
a list of wrongs never done, forty-two
gods, a spell spoken to the heart,

10
00:00:43,813 --> 00:00:47,973
and the question of whether
anyone was expected to fail.

11
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:58,995
First, there was no
single Book of the Dead.

12
00:00:59,295 --> 00:01:03,805
These texts were known by phrases
such as going forth by day.

13
00:01:04,105 --> 00:01:07,912
In the New Kingdom, selections
of these spells came to be

14
00:01:07,912 --> 00:01:11,378
written on papyrus scrolls
and buried with the dead.

15
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:15,857
The choice and order of spells
varied from scroll to scroll.

16
00:01:16,157 --> 00:01:21,201
On some, the owner's name was written
in later, in spaces left blank.

17
00:01:21,501 --> 00:01:24,106
Some scrolls held only a few spells.

18
00:01:24,406 --> 00:01:27,237
Others held more than a hundred and fifty.

19
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,918
The name Book of the Dead is modern.

20
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:38,071
In 1842, the German scholar Karl
Richard Lepsius published a long

21
00:01:38,071 --> 00:01:43,467
papyrus kept in Turin, under
the title the Book of the Dead.

22
00:01:43,767 --> 00:01:46,761
His numbering of its spells
became the basis

23
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:49,547
of the numbers scholars still use today.

24
00:01:49,847 --> 00:01:54,921
The judgment we are looking
for is mainly in Spell 125.

25
00:01:56,733 --> 00:02:03,215
Spell 125 takes the dead person into
a great hall, the Hall of the Two Truths.

26
00:02:03,515 --> 00:02:06,534
There, the dead person
speaks for themselves.

27
00:02:06,834 --> 00:02:11,387
They greet the great god, the lord
of truth, and say that they know him.

28
00:02:11,687 --> 00:02:14,154
Then they list the wrongs they never did.

29
00:02:15,467 --> 00:02:18,327
Scholars call this
the Negative Confession.

30
00:02:18,627 --> 00:02:20,892
Its lines are short and plain.

31
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:25,959
Here are a few, from different versions,
in an old English translation.

32
00:02:26,258 --> 00:02:27,946
I have not stolen.

33
00:02:28,246 --> 00:02:30,070
I have done no murder.

34
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:32,290
I have spoken no lies.

35
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:34,606
I have made no man to weep.

36
00:02:34,906 --> 00:02:37,756
I have not turned back
water at its springtide.

37
00:02:40,867 --> 00:02:44,041
Then the dead person
turns to forty-two gods,

38
00:02:44,041 --> 00:02:47,071
and greets each one by a name and a place.

39
00:02:47,371 --> 00:02:51,119
The first is greeted as the one
whose strides are long,

40
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:55,560
who comes forth from Annu, the city
the Greeks called Heliopolis.

41
00:02:55,860 --> 00:03:00,573
To each god, the dead person names
one more wrong they did not do.

42
00:03:00,873 --> 00:03:02,729
Knowing the names mattered.

43
00:03:03,029 --> 00:03:06,729
One version ends, I know
you, and I know your names.

44
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:16,473
But what if the heart disagreed?

45
00:03:16,773 --> 00:03:21,102
For the Egyptians, the heart
was the center of thought and feeling.

46
00:03:21,402 --> 00:03:24,910
It was believed to remember
what a person had done.

47
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:29,593
So another spell, Spell 30B,
speaks directly to the heart.

48
00:03:29,893 --> 00:03:33,887
In an old translation, it
begins, My heart my mother.

49
00:03:34,187 --> 00:03:38,070
It asks that nothing oppose
the dead person at the judgment,

50
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:42,941
and that the heart not be parted
from them before the keeper of the scales.

51
00:03:43,241 --> 00:03:48,593
Modern translators read it as a plea that
the heart not speak against its owner.

52
00:03:54,733 --> 00:03:59,822
This spell was often inscribed
on a heart scarab, an amulet in the shape

53
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:04,695
of a beetle, and such scarabs
were often placed on the mummy's chest.

54
00:04:04,995 --> 00:04:08,464
On this one, with a human head, the museum

55
00:04:08,464 --> 00:04:12,511
identifies the ten lines
on the base as Spell 30B.

56
00:04:12,811 --> 00:04:15,907
The dead person did not
face the scale alone.

57
00:04:16,207 --> 00:04:18,619
They brought their own
words for the heart.

58
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:23,341
Now look again at the scale.

59
00:04:23,641 --> 00:04:26,779
Here, the heart is not shown
lighter than the feather.

60
00:04:27,079 --> 00:04:29,341
The two sides hang in balance.

61
00:04:29,641 --> 00:04:33,702
Museum descriptions speak
of the heart balancing with Maat.

62
00:04:34,002 --> 00:04:36,095
Thoth writes down the result.

63
00:04:36,395 --> 00:04:39,222
And nearby waits Ammit, the Devourer.

64
00:04:39,522 --> 00:04:43,025
One old description
calls her part crocodile,

65
00:04:43,025 --> 00:04:45,652
part lion, and part hippopotamus.

66
00:04:47,833 --> 00:04:50,963
But papyri made to help
the dead live on are

67
00:04:50,963 --> 00:04:54,093
unlikely to show
their owner being devoured.

68
00:04:54,393 --> 00:04:57,199
In the text, the heart is found true,

69
00:04:57,199 --> 00:05:00,991
and the dead person is allowed
to go before Osiris.

70
00:05:01,291 --> 00:05:04,533
The scroll was made to help
secure a good outcome.

71
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:08,999
After their names, the dead
were often called true of voice.

72
00:05:14,367 --> 00:05:17,152
So, could you fail
the Weighing of the Heart?

73
00:05:17,452 --> 00:05:19,265
As a fear, yes.

74
00:05:19,565 --> 00:05:21,571
That is why Ammit is there.

75
00:05:21,871 --> 00:05:25,238
But the Book of the Dead
was made to help you pass.

76
00:05:25,538 --> 00:05:29,018
And its denials also describe a good life:

77
00:05:29,018 --> 00:05:33,078
do not steal, do not lie,
do not make others weep.

78
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:40,794
Every story has an origin.

79
00:05:41,094 --> 00:05:44,266
Sometimes, it begins
with a heart, a feather,

80
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:46,861
and a few words prepared in advance.

81
00:05:49,233 --> 00:05:54,393
This video is based on historical
texts and public museum collections.

82
00:05:54,693 --> 00:05:57,413
The narration uses synthetic speech.

83
00:05:57,713 --> 00:06:01,669
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

84
00:06:01,969 --> 00:06:04,737
If you enjoyed this story, please like

85
00:06:04,737 --> 00:06:08,089
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

86
00:06:08,389 --> 00:06:10,162
Thank you for watching.
